+/*
+ * BMP 102: a faulted memory read window is re-issued before it is reported.
+ *
+ * The debug module on these parts drops the odd transfer - a mis-decoded DMI frame on the
+ * bit-banged SDI transport is one way (riscv_fault.md §10.2/§12), a dropped DMI write is
+ * another (the V006 measurements counted 26 lost data1 writes in 7638 transfers) - and the
+ * path then reports a failure that has nothing to do with the address: measured on a
+ * CH32V003 over SDI, one 13 KB .text window in three faults, and the same window read again
+ * comes back clean, twice, with every section matching.
+ *
+ * A faulted window is therefore read again instead of being handed to the caller: the
+ * alternative is GDB aborting a section load or a compare-sections verify on a single
+ * dropped frame. This costs a healthy target nothing - the loop is only entered after a
+ * path has already set hart->status, so the first pass, and every successful read on every
+ * part, is exactly the traffic it was before - and it costs a *faulted* window the same
+ * window again, which is what the depth below is about.
+ *
+ * Why eight attempts: the fault rate is per *word* (~1e-4 on a 13 KB read in one window in
+ * three, 3300 words), so a window re-issued once still has most of its original chance of
+ * faulting again - a window that faults is not a window that is broken. Eight attempts take
+ * the chance of a *reported* failure from ~0.3 to ~1e-4, which is what makes a bulk read of
+ * a whole section trustworthy rather than usually-working. Re-issuing is bounded and only
+ * ever on the path where the failure was *detected* (the caller got an error, not the
+ * previous answer - F8 in riscv_fault.md), so a genuinely unreadable address pays eight
+ * accesses and then reports what the first one found.
+ *
+ * Reads only, all three read paths (system bus, program buffer and abstract). A write is not
+ * idempotent - a faulted write may or may not have landed, and re-issuing one is what would
+ * corrupt a flash access. Re-issuing a *read* through the program buffer re-runs a load and
+ * a post-increment; BMP 100 already puts a0 and a1 back whatever the run did, so the retry
+ * cannot leave the hart in a different state than the attempt before it did.
+ */
+/*
